// This keyword - It refers to the current object
     the specific object whose method or constructor is currently executing .
     Using this.name explicitly tells java compiler "I mean the instance variable of this object not the parameter."
     public void set_data(String name , String color , int gears , int price){
           this.name = name;
           this.color = color;
           this.gears = gears;
           this.price = price; }

// Construction -

// 1. It is a member function of a class
// 2. It has the same name as name of the class
// 3. It has no return type , so cannot use return keyword
// 4. It must be an instance member function that is it can never be static

// How to call constructor

// 1. Constructor is implicitly invoked just after the creation of an object
// 2. Constructor is used to solve the problem of Initialization

// Types of constructor

// 1. Default Constructor ->

    a. Object ban ne par constructor automatically call hote hain .
    b. Compiler constructor banate hain , agar hum constructor nhi bnate hn .
    c. Compiler jo constructor banate hn usme koi argument nhi pass hota
    d. yadi hum 1 bhi constructor banate hn to compiler apne se koi constructor nhi bnata
    e. Jab hum koi bhi constructor nhi banate hn to compiler apne se 1 constructor banata h
        1. default constructor

    f. jab hum koi bhi constructor banate hn (exact copy constructor ) tab compiler sirf copy constructor banata h (Invalid for Java)
    g. jab hum copy constructor banate hn tab compiler koi bhi constructor nhi banata
    h. Java mein agar copy constructor banana hai to use sirf user hi bna sakta hai . java compiler nhi bna sakta

// 2. Parameterized Constructor -> Parameters are given by user

// 3. Copy Constructor ->

    ek object ke value ko dusre mein copy karne ko copy constructor kehte hn .

// Access Specifiers / Access Modifiers / Visibility Modes

    1. Private - Accessible only within the class
    2. Protected - Accessible within the class and derived class
    3. Public - Accessible from anywhere and by anyone

// The Four Access Specifiers in Java

//  specifier        same class       same package     subclass(different package)     different package(non-subclass)

    private         yes             No                  No                             NO
    default        Yes             yes                 No                             NO
    Protected       yes             yes                 yes                            No
    public          yes             yes                 yes                            yes


// The Problem : Data Shared Across ALl Objects
    Every object created from a class gets its own copy of instance variables . But sometimes , we need a
    value that should be shared by all objects - the same single copy , used and updated by every object collectively

    Example Scenario : Suppose we want to count how many student objects have been created so far , across the
    entire program , if count were a regular instance variable , each object would have its own seperate count ,
    always starting at 0 - that wont work for a shared , running total .
    This is exactly what the static keyboard solves .

// Static Variable ( class variables )

    A static variable belongs to the class itself , not to any individual object .
    There is only one copy of it , shared by all objects of the class .

    class student {
        String name ;
        static int studentCount = 0;

        Student(String name){
            this.name = name ;
            studentCount ++;
        }
    }
    public class main {
        public static void main(String[] args){
            Student s1 = new Student("Ravi");
            Student s2 = new Student("Abhey");

            System.out.println("Total Students :" + student.studentCount);
        }
    }

    // Key Observations :
    -> studentCount is accessed using the class name : Student.studentCount(this is the preferred way),
       though s1.studentCount also technically works .
    -> All three objects share and update the same studentCount - it isn't resent per object .


// Static Methods
   A static method also belongs to the class , not to any specific object . It can be called directly using the class
   name , without creating an object first .

   Class MathUtils {
        static int square(int n){
            return n*n;
        }
   }
   public class Main{
        public static void main(String[] args){
            int result = MathUtils.square(5);
            System.out.println(result);
        }
   }

   This is exactly why main itself is static : public static void main(...) needs to run before any object of the
   class even exists , so the JVM must be able to call it directly on the class .

// Important Restriction : Static Methods Cannot Access Instance Members Directly

    Class Student{
        String name ;
        static int count ;

        static void show(){
            System.out.println(count);   // ok - static can  access static
            // System..out.println(name);  // error , cannot access instance variable
        }
    }

    why ? A static method might run before any object exists at all .
    Since name only exists once an object is created , there's no gauranteed name for the static method to refer to .
    Static methods can only directly access other static members .

// Static vs Instance - Summary table

                                        Instance(non static)            static

             belongs to                 each individual object          the class itself
             copies                     one copy per object             one single copy , shared by all
             Accessed via               objectName.member               className.member (preffered)
             can access Instance
             members ?                   yes                            No , not directly
             Exists                     only after object creation      Exists as soon as the class is loaded
                                        (new)



// Static Blocks
   What is Static Block ?
   A static block is a block of code that runs exactly once , when the class is first loaded into memory - even before
   the main method runs , and before any object is created . Its mainly used to initialize static variables with some
   computation or logic ( not just a simple direct assignment) .

   Syntax and Example

   class Configuration{
        static String appname ;
        static int version ;

        // static block
        static {
            system.out.println("Static block executing ..");
            appname = "StudentManager";
            version = 1;
        }
   }
   public class Main{
        public static void main(String[] args){
            System.out.println("Main method started");
            System.out.println(configuration.appname);
            System.out.println(configuration.version);
        }
   }

   output :
   static block executing ...
   Main method started
   studentManager
   1

   Notice : "Static block executing ..." prints before "Main method started" -
            because the static block runs automatically the moment the configuration class is loaded
            ( which happens as soon as its referenced ) , even though main is the method we "Start" the program from .

// Why use a Static Block instead of direct assignment ?

    for simple values , direct assignment is enough :

    eg ->       static int version = 1;     // simple , no static block needed

    But static blocks are useful when initialization requires some logic - loops ,conditions , exception handling , or
    reading external configuration - things a single line assignment cannot express .

    class constants{
        static int[] evenNumbers = new int[5];

        static {
            for(int i = 0;i<5;i++){
                evenNumbers[i] = i * 2;   // computed initialization : 0,2,4,6,8,10
            }
        }
    }

//  Order of Execution ( Important Concept )

    when a class is loaded , java executes things in this order :
    1. Static Variables and static blocks - run once ,in the order they appear in the code ,
     when the class is first loaded .
    2. Instance variables and instance initializer blocks , then constructor - run every time a new object is created
    3. main() method ( If this is the class being run ) executes as part of this overall loading process .

// Encapsulation ->
    It is an important pillar of oops that binds together the data(variables) & the methods that manipulates
    the data & that keeps the data safe from outside interference & misuse .
    class user{
        private string username;
        private int password;

        user(string username , int password){
            this.username = username;
            this.password = password;
        }
        public int getpassword(){
            return password;
        }
        public string getusername(){
            return username;
        }
        public class encapsulation{
            psvma (s[] args){
                user obj = new user("atagi" , 123445);
                string username = obj.getusername();
                int pass = obj.getpassword();
                sop("username & password of your account is" + username +" & "+ password);
            }
        }
    }

